The four design changes have now been made in the document:

1. REPOSITORY ADMISSION RULES. Autonomous merge exists only for ADMITTED repos, and admission is a recorded per-repo decision proving a merge does nothing but change source: no deploy-on-merge, repo is not itself the live product, no machine auto-pulls main, no push/PR publishes packages or updates infrastructure or contacts outside systems, no automation with production secrets, no automation running agent code before the gauntlet. Any "yes" → automation disabled, repo excluded, or the owner's authenticated tap is explicitly the manual deploy step. Projects are an explicit allowlist; the EMR is structurally refused (a new recorded owner decision, not a config edit, would be required). The GitHub broker refuses a branch push unless the admission record shows that branch automation holds no valuable credentials and has no production effect.

2. EXCLUSIONS BY RULE. No connected tool may hold production credentials, cloud administration, publishing authority, repository administration, branch-rule bypass, live-database access, or patient data — a rule, not per-tool documentation. The GitHub broker is unable (not just untasked) to change repo settings, branch rules, releases, packages, deployment environments, or repos outside the allowlist.

3. CORRECTED CLAIMS AND BENCHMARK. The document now states: git reverses source, not consequences (with the examples you gave); the harness is "safer in blast radius, more autonomous" than terminal use — not "no more dangerous"; the extra autonomy risk lands on unwanted-but-source-reversible merges and routine spend. The "already built and regression-tested" credit for the record guards was removed — the document now states plainly that the live database still runs the old weaker rules and no credit is claimed until it is migrated and re-attacked, and that review-to-version binding is not yet enforced in the build. Your expanded residual-risk list (manual deploy of fooled code, source-revert ≠ consequence-revert, broker/account/provider compromise, cumulative merges, availability, voice audio to the provider, licensing) was folded in for explicit owner acceptance.

4. VOICE INTENT RESOLVED. You offered: authenticated non-voice confirmation before voice-originated merges, OR explicit owner acceptance of unwanted-but-reversible merges. The owner chose fully hands-free and the document records this as OWNER DECISION E, with its blast radius bounded by admission rules (a wrong merge changes source in a repo where merges have no consequences beyond source).

Your scope-leak findings were also folded into the decommissioning list as verified-dead requirements: the Netlify deploy command, the Cloudflare tunnel and its startup machinery, the default-password installer, every direct host launch of coding models (voice and text paths included), the provider key in the web service, and the live-database migration.

Your build-must-prove list is adopted as the build-phase acceptance test (the document's numbered attack list was extended with admission-rule and broker-scope attacks).

