    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	## The threat model
    88	
    89	Not the owner. The real threats, in order:
    90	
    91	1. **An agent doing something nobody asked for.**
    92	2. **Prompt injection** — poisoned text in a file, dependency, issue, or web page turns
    93	   a fooled-but-authorized agent into an attacker's hands. Load-bearing, because the
    94	   agent is *allowed* to read the repo.
    95	3. **Mishearing** — road noise, a passenger, a bad transcript.
    96	4. **Code that passes honest review** (the round-4 finding, still true at this scope):
    97	   a subtle payload can survive the gauntlet and land in a repo. What contains it here
    98	   is not review but *consequence limits*: it lands in a git-reversible repo, in a
    99	   project with no customer data, with no credentials to steal in the workspace, no
   100	   production to reach, and a record of exactly which job produced it.
   101	
   102	## What protects against those threats — the doors, honestly labeled
   103	
   104	| Protection | Kind | What it actually stops |
   105	|---|---|---|
   106	| GitHub branch protection | Server-side boundary | History rewrite and direct pushes to protected branches; enforces tap-to-merge where dialed |
   107	| Credentials absent from agent reach | Absence | Exfiltration and misuse of what isn't there |
   108	| The sealed workspace (VM) | Kernel/hypervisor boundary | Agents touching the owner's files, business, or network |
   109	| Brokered GitHub/provider access | Narrow authority | Agents holding reusable keys; runaway spend at brokered providers |
   110	| Provider-side hard caps | External boundary | Spend past the cap, where the provider offers a real one |
   111	| The gauntlet + job record | Quality gate + audit | Bad work landing *unexamined*; "what did the overnight run do?" going unanswerable |
   112	| Git itself | Reversibility of SOURCE | Makes a bad merge usually recoverable as a source change |
   113	
   114	The table is the design's honesty mechanism: the first five are boundaries; the
   115	gauntlet is explicitly *not* one (round 4's lesson). And git's reversibility is stated
   116	precisely: **git reverses source, not consequences.** It cannot unpublish a leaked
   117	secret, undo automation that already ran, retract a downloaded package, or repair
   118	damaged live data — which is exactly why repos where a merge *has* consequences are
   119	refused admission to autonomous merge.
   120	
   121	---
   122	
   123	## Where agents run — the sealed workspace
   124	
   125	**DECIDED (Neill, 2026-07-15): a dedicated VM on this Mac**, migratable to separate
   126	hardware by config. The profile keeps everything cheap-and-real from round 6; the
   127	honest caveat stands — this protects against ordinary containment failures, and the
   128	owner knowingly accepts the residual risk of hostile execution sharing his business
   129	Mac:
   130	
   131	- No shared folders, clipboard, or device passthrough; no credential connections; no
   132	  host-management channel; no Tailscale identity inside; no inbound connections.
   133	- Outgoing traffic only through the named brokers, enforced by a host-side firewall the
   134	  guest cannot reconfigure.
   135	- Fixed-size disk; hard CPU, memory, process, time, and network limits — an agent must
   136	  not be able to brick the Mac by exhaustion. Disk-full/memory/flood/crash responses
   137	  tested.
   138	- Isolated clones per agent — never shared working copies (a git worktree shares
   139	  history and hooks; a clone doesn't).
   140	- The profile covers every runner (Claude Code, Codex, Ollama), child process, hook,
   141	  MCP server, scheduled job, retry, and recovery path. Local MCP servers run inside.
   142	  Connected tools are governed by an **exclusion rule, not per-tool documentation**:
   143	  no connected tool may hold production credentials, cloud administration, publishing
   144	  authority, repository administration, branch-rule bypass, live-database access, or
   145	  patient data. A tool that would recreate the removed release authority is refused,
   146	  period.
   147	- Kernel sandbox on, strict, as the second layer inside the VM. Startup checks detect
   148	  drift; the VM is the wall.
   149	- Prompt security updates for the Mac and VM software; snapshots and crash dumps
   150	  treated as sensitive.
   151	
   152	## No keys in agent hands — the brokers
   153	
   154	- **GitHub broker.** Agents don't hold GitHub credentials. They hand finished work to
   155	  the broker, which pushes the branch, opens the PR, and — on admitted repos — merges
   156	  after the gauntlet passes. Its token has the narrowest scope GitHub allows, and the
   157	  broker is **unable** (not just untasked) to change repository settings, branch
   158	  rules, releases, packages, deployment environments, or repos outside the allowlist.
   159	  Because pushing a branch can itself start repository automation *before* the
   160	  gauntlet, **the broker refuses the push** unless that automation is known (from the
   161	  admission record) to hold no valuable credentials, no broad repo authority, and no
   162	  production effect. Repos dialed to tap-to-merge are enforced by branch protection,
   163	  not by the broker's good behavior.
   164	- **Provider broker.** Agent runners get no reusable API or subscription credentials;
   165	  the broker applies per-job budget *before* each call and is the only path to a
   166	  provider. Hard provider-side caps wherever offered — stated honestly: only the
   167	  provider's own cap is a real financial boundary; elsewhere the broker is a strong
   168	  mitigation. Provider *responses* are untrusted input.
   169	- Subscription runners that technically require login material inside their
   170	  environment are treated as holding a credential and narrowed accordingly — named,
   171	  not hidden.
   172	
   173	## The gauntlet and the record
   174	
   175	- Every dispatched job gets a row; no silent work. Reviews, verdicts, and evidence
   176	  bind to the exact commit they examined (the round-3 lesson: a verdict that outlives
   177	  the code it reviewed is worthless). A fail condemns a version, not the job forever;
   178	  verdicts can't be deleted or edited pass-ward. **Honest status (Sol round 7): these
   179	  rules are hardened in the schema source but the LIVE database still runs the old,
