   291	
   292	**Residual risks, named for the owner to accept knowingly** (expanded per Sol round
   293	7 — these are accepted, not contained):
   294	
   295	- A subtle payload can pass honest review and land in a repo — contained by source
   296	  reversibility, admission rules, absence of credentials/production/customer data in
   297	  scope, and the audit trail; not prevented. **If the owner later manually deploys
   298	  that code, it can harm production — the manual step stops autonomous release; it
   299	  does not make the code correct.**
   300	- **Reverting source cannot undo consequences**: a disclosed secret, corrupted live
   301	  data, a published package, or an external action already taken stays done.
   302	- Source code and project content necessarily flow to the model providers the seats
   303	  use; an injected instruction could deliberately include private project material in
   304	  a legitimate request — and encoded exfiltration through an allowed channel cannot be
   305	  reliably scanned away. Scope (no EMR, no customer data) is the mitigation.
   306	  **Continuous voice additionally sends the owner's speech — and possibly nearby
   307	  conversation — to the voice provider.**
   308	- An always-on harness acts without the owner watching; misheard or ambient speech can
   309	  start work and (Decision E) merge it. The record, budgets, admission rules, and
   310	  voice status reports are the compensations.
   311	- **Many individually harmless merges can accumulate into a dangerous whole that no
   312	  single review sees** — the audit trail is the recovery tool, not a preventer.
   313	- A bug or compromise in a broker, a GitHub account, a model provider, a connected
   314	  tool, or a software update could grant more authority than intended; the record can
   315	  be incomplete or written falsely by a compromised trusted service.
   316	- Same-Mac VM escape via hypervisor bug: mitigated by patching; migration to separate
   317	  hardware is a config change.
   318	- A compromised or spoofed voice/text session can create work and burn routine budget
   319	  (never open doors).
   320	- **Availability is not guaranteed**: provider outages, exhausted subscription
   321	  allowances, queue flooding, or a failed broker can stop the harness without damage.
   322	- Agent-written code can carry licensing or ownership problems even when it works.
   323	
   324	---
   325	
   326	## ✅ OWNER DECISIONS — record
   327	
   328	- **A (2026-07-15/16):** made under the autonomous-release framing; superseded by D.
   329	  Its residue: the gauntlet always runs, and the per-repo tap-to-merge dial exists.
   330	- **B (2026-07-15):** agents run in a dedicated VM on this Mac; residual same-Mac risk
   331	  accepted; migration stays a config change.
   332	- **C (2026-07-15):** the PHI-bearing EMR is out of scope; revisit requires
   333	  attack-tested medical-data egress controls first.
   334	- **D (2026-07-17):** the proportionality reset. The harness is a development harness
   335	  benchmarked honestly against the owner's daily terminal use of coding agents (safer
   336	  in blast radius, more autonomous); agents merge to ADMITTED repos autonomously after
   337	  the gauntlet; production deployment stays manual; irreversible powers stay behind
   338	  server-side and physical doors; Sol names residual risks rather than certifying
   339	  impossibility.
   340	- **E (2026-07-17):** voice-originated jobs merge autonomously like typed ones. Neill
   341	  knowingly accepts that misheard or ambient speech can produce unwanted,
   342	  source-reversible merges in admitted repos, rather than adding a confirm-intent tap.

codex
The revised document now contains all four topics, but the critical question is whether its “admitted repository” label is protected from the agents it is meant to constrain. I’m tracing who can create or change that admission record and whether a normal branch push can have side effects before review.
