     1	# Voice-First Multi-Model Agent Harness — Build Spec
     2	
     3	> **Status: OWNER-DELIVERED REBOOT SPEC (2026-07-11, verbatim).** Neill: "I essentially have an entirely new spec for chief command.. I literally probably want to start over with it." This supersedes prior CC plans as the direction of record pending the design conversation. The existing CHIEF_COMMAND_V3_SPEC.md and running v1 backend remain for reference/salvage — nothing deleted.
     4	
     5	**Project codename:** Chief Command v2 (rename at will)
     6	**Owner:** Neill
     7	**Prime directive:** Two-way voice is the product. Everything else is plumbing behind it. But the build order is text-first, voice-second — the voice layer must be a swappable skin over a harness that works fully via text, so a voice problem can never block the whole project again.
     8	
     9	---
    10	
    11	## 1. What this is
    12	
    13	An always-on personal agent orchestration system. Neill talks to it (push-to-talk, from his phone anywhere or at his desk) and it routes work to a stack of AI models: a frontier orchestrator, an autonomous coding workhorse, a free local grinder, and an escalation reviewer. It runs builds, reviews its own output through a multi-agent gauntlet, and reports back by voice.
    14	
    15	## 2. Hardware & environment
    16	
    17	- **Phase 0 host:** Neill's current MacBook Pro (Apple Silicon). Everything must run here first. Detect available RAM at setup and size the local model accordingly (see §5.3).
    18	- **Phase 2+ host:** a Mac Studio (target ~64GB M4 Max) to be purchased later. **Hard requirement: the entire system must be hardware-agnostic** — one config file change (hostnames/endpoints) migrates it. No machine-specific paths hardcoded.
    19	- **OS:** macOS. Services managed via `launchd` so they survive reboots.
    20	- **Networking:** Tailscale mesh. The harness binds to the tailnet, never the public internet. Phone and MacBook reach it via Tailscale hostname. No port forwarding, no exposed services.
    21	
    22	## 3. Core harness: OpenClaw
    23	
    24	OpenClaw is the orchestration backbone — gateway, session management, agent memory, and the routing layer to all model providers.
    25	
    26	Requirements:
    27	- Install pinned to a specific version. **No auto-updates.** Version bumps are deliberate, tested events (the Gemini CLI shutdown is the cautionary tale — vendor and tooling behavior changes under you).
    28	- All provider connections defined in config, each swappable independently.
    29	- Runs as a persistent daemon under `launchd`.
    30	- Expose a local HTTP API + web UI over the tailnet (this is what the phone/desktop clients hit).
    31	- Text chat interface is the first deliverable and permanent fallback. If voice dies, text always works.
    32	
    33	## 4. Model providers & routing
    34	
    35	Four seats. Each one behind a named role in config so providers can be swapped without touching orchestration logic.
    36	
    37	### 4.1 Orchestrator — OpenAI Codex (ChatGPT subscription)
    38	- Connect via Codex's official OAuth route for third-party frameworks (subscription-based, no API billing). This is sanctioned by OpenAI.
    39	- Role: top-level planning, task decomposition, structured builds, running the reviewer gauntlet (§6).
    40	- Uses Codex CLI's native subagent system (GA since March 2026): custom agents as TOML files in `~/.codex/agents/`, up to 8 parallel workers, `max_depth = 1`.
    41	- Model routing within Codex: flagship model (GPT-5.5 / 5.6 when available) for orchestration and judgment; mini-tier model for cheap parallel subagent fanout.
    42	
    43	### 4.2 Autonomous workhorse — Grok Build (SuperGrok subscription)
    44	- xAI explicitly sanctions headless (`-p`) and third-party-harness use on subscription, including OpenClaw via OAuth. Use it for exactly that: unattended, long-running build jobs dispatched by the harness.
    45	- Role: volume autonomous coding — migrations, test backfills, large refactors. Supports up to 8 parallel subagents in git worktrees.
    46	- Treat xAI's permissiveness as revocable. Isolate behind the provider abstraction like everyone else.
    47	
    48	### 4.3 Local grinder — Ollama (free, 24/7)
    49	- Serve via Ollama's network API so any machine on the tailnet can use it.
    50	- Model sizing by available RAM:
    51	  - MacBook Pro phase: whatever fits comfortably — Qwen3-Coder 30B class if 36GB+, otherwise a 7–14B coder. It's a junior dev either way.
    52	  - Studio phase (64GB): Qwen3-Coder 30B resident full-time alongside the voice stack.
    53	- Role: overnight/background grinding — scaffolding, boilerplate, test writing, first-draft fixes. **Design rule: local output never ships without a subscription-tier review pass.**
    54	
    55	### 4.4 Escalation reviewer — Claude (Max subscription + credit pool)
    56	- Two modes, and the distinction matters:
    57	  - **Interactive (subscription, effectively free):** Neill opens a Claude Code session (locally or via SSH) and reviews the gauntlet's flagged queue himself with Claude. Human-initiated = subscription limits.
    58	  - **Headless (credit pool):** the harness may invoke `claude -p` for automated deep reviews, drawing on the Max plan's included monthly agent credits. Budget-cap this in config (e.g., N automated Claude reviews/day) so it never silently overruns the free allotment.
    59	- Role: highest-judgment work — architecture review, contested reviewer verdicts, the bug the local model looped on three times.
    60	- This seat is optional/droppable. The system must run fully without it.
    61	
    62	### 4.5 Explicitly excluded
    63	- **Google/Gemini:** ToS prohibits third-party OAuth use (names OpenClaw specifically), and consumer Gemini CLI access ended June 18, 2026. Do not integrate. Revisit only via paid API keys if ever needed.
    64	
    65	## 5. Voice layer (the product)
    66	
    67	### 5.1 The core architecture decision — voice skin ≠ brain
    68	The voice layer and the orchestrator are **separate**. A fast, local voice front-end handles listening and speaking; it makes **tool calls** to the GPT-5.6 orchestrator (Codex, §4.1) which holds the harness memory, subagents, and reasoning. You are talking *to your actual orchestrator* — the local pipeline is just its ears and mouth. This is what kills the Chief Command latency wound: the voice layer acknowledges in ~300ms ("on it, kicking that off") while 5.6 reasons and builds underneath. Perceived latency is the voice layer's, not the builder's.
    69	
    70	This means you get 5.6's full intelligence — interrogating a build, pushing back, inspecting its own work — with local, free, low-latency voice on top. The orchestrator is never a dumbed-down voice model; it's the real brain with all the context and subagents.
    71	
    72	### 5.2 Interaction model
    73	- **Push-to-talk** on all clients. Hold/tap mic button → speak → release → response. No wake word, no open mic in v1.
    74	- Full duplex feel via streaming: user should hear the start of the reply in **under ~1.5 seconds** from releasing the button. That's the latency budget and the acceptance test.
    75	
    76	### 5.3 Pipeline (all local, all free) — the chosen path
    77	Decision: **local Whisper + Kokoro, NOT OpenAI realtime voice.** Rationale is cost at Neill's usage pattern (a couple hours/day, indefinitely):
    78	- OpenAI's realtime voice (gpt-realtime-2) does **not** run on the ChatGPT/Codex subscription — it's separate metered OpenAI Platform API billing (~$0.18–0.46/min for the full model). At 2 hrs/day that's roughly **$700–1000+/month**. Even gpt-realtime-mini with prompt caching (~$0.05–0.10/min) lands around **$150–250/month** for daily hours-long use. Not worth it for a single-user personal builder.
    79	- Local pipeline = **$0**, runs on the machine being bought anyway, and the streaming design below already hits the ~1.5s target. The only thing given up is the last increment of speech-to-speech polish/barge-in — irrelevant for "kick off and talk through a build."
    80	
    81	Pipeline:
    82	- **STT:** faster-whisper (or whisper.cpp) running on the host. Streaming transcription — begin transcribing as audio arrives, don't wait for end-of-utterance.
    83	- **TTS:** Kokoro. **Sentence-chunked synthesis:** begin playback the moment the first sentence of the response is ready while the rest generates. This is the single most important latency decision — perceived latency = time-to-first-sentence, not total response time.
    84	- **Plumbing:** use Pipecat (or equivalent mature framework) for the streaming/turn-taking pipeline rather than hand-wiring WebSocket audio. Chief Command v1 died hand-wiring this; don't repeat it.
    85	- Voice responses should be *spoken-length* — the harness prompts models for a short verbal summary, with full detail written to the session log/text UI.
    86	- **Optional swap-in (later):** the voice front-end sits behind the same provider abstraction as everything else, so gpt-realtime-mini can be A/B'd against the local stack if you ever want to feel the difference. Metered, off by default, per-session cost cap if enabled. Reserve paid realtime voice for short transactional interactions (Jess's territory), never hours-long builder sessions.
    87	
    88	- **Phone (primary):** a mobile web app (PWA) served by the harness over Tailscale. Big push-to-talk button, streaming audio out, text transcript visible, text input as fallback. Native wrapper only if PWA mic handling on iOS proves unreliable — evaluate honestly and early, since iOS Safari mic/background-audio quirks are the likeliest pain point.
    89	- **Desktop:** same web UI, plus optional global-hotkey push-to-talk menubar utility (later phase).
    90	
    91	## 6. Agent gauntlet (ported from Claude Code)
    92	
    93	Neill has an existing Claude Code setup: orchestrator + ~10 specialized agents, a 6-reviewer gauntlet, and a tester agent. Port it:
    94	
    95	- Convert Claude Code agent markdown definitions → Codex TOML agents (same instructions, new wrapper). Keep the originals so Claude Code remains usable in parallel.
    96	- `AGENTS.md` is the cross-agent standard — Codex, Grok Build, and Claude Code all read it. Single source of truth for project conventions. (Codex also reads CLAUDE.md as fallback; converge on AGENTS.md.)
    97	- SKILL.md skills are portable across clients; carry them over as-is.
    98	- Gauntlet flow: builder produces work → up to 6 reviewers run **in parallel** as Codex subagents (mini-tier models) → tester agent validates → disagreements or flagged items escalate to the Claude seat or to Neill's interactive review queue.
    99	- **Model diversity rule:** reviewer panel should draw from at least two model families (e.g., GPT-mini reviewers + Grok reviewer + local reviewer). Different models miss different bugs.
   100	- Codex does not auto-spawn custom agents — delegation must be written explicitly into the workflow prompts / AGENTS.md.
   101	
   102	## 7. Memory & state
   103	
   104	- Port Neill's existing memory files (CLAUDE.md / MEMORY.md-style markdown) into a harness-level `memory/` directory loaded into orchestrator context.
   105	- Session state, job queue, and build history in SQLite on the host.
   106	- Every dispatched job gets a persistent record: what was asked, which provider ran it, gauntlet verdicts, final status. Voice query "what did the overnight run do?" must be answerable from this.
   107	- Git as audit trail for everything the agents produce.
   108	
   109	## 8. Build phases & acceptance criteria
   110	
   111	**Phase 1 — Text harness (the machine).**
   112	OpenClaw daemon on MacBook Pro; Codex OAuth connected; Ollama serving a local model; web UI reachable from phone over Tailscale. ✅ *Accept: from the phone, type "have the local model write X, then have Codex review it" and it happens, with the job recorded.*
   113	
   114	**Phase 2 — Voice loop, local, wired to the orchestrator.**
   115	Whisper + Kokoro + Pipecat pipeline on the desktop client, making tool calls into the GPT-5.6 orchestrator with full harness memory. ✅ *Accept: push-to-talk at desk, talk through a build with the orchestrator (not a stand-in), hear the answer begin in <1.5s while heavy work runs underneath.*
   116	
   117	**Phase 3 — Voice from phone.**
   118	PWA push-to-talk over Tailscale. ✅ *Accept: from the golf course parking lot, dispatch a build by voice and hear confirmation.*
   119	
   120	**Phase 4 — Autonomy & gauntlet.**
   121	Grok Build seat connected; full reviewer gauntlet ported; overnight queue running; Claude escalation wired with credit budget cap. ✅ *Accept: queue 3 tasks at night by voice; wake to a triaged, gauntlet-reviewed PR stack and a spoken morning summary on request.*
   122	
   123	**Phase 5 — Studio migration.**
   124	Buy the Studio (or M5 Ultra, pending October). Migration = config change + data copy. ✅ *Accept: full system live on Studio in under a half day; MacBook Pro demoted to client.*
   125	
   126	## 9. Risks & standing constraints
   127	
   128	- **Vendor policy drift is the #1 risk.** Anthropic already moved headless to credits; Google killed consumer CLI access; xAI's openness is young. Mitigation: provider abstraction, pinned versions, no single-vendor dependency, text fallback always.
   129	- **Claude headless = credit pool, always.** Never architect around gray-area workarounds (e.g., puppeting interactive sessions programmatically) — Anthropic bans aggressive circumvention, and the sanctioned path is cheap anyway.
   130	- **iOS PWA audio quirks** — likeliest engineering pain in Phase 3; prototype early.
   131	- **Local model overconfidence** — enforce the never-ship-unreviewed rule in the pipeline itself, not by convention.
   132	- **Token burn** — parallel gauntlets multiply usage; instrument per-provider usage counters from day one and surface them in the UI.
   133	
   134	## 10. External connection: Jess (future)
   135	
   136	Jess (Neill's personal assistant, built separately on the Google/Gemini stack in her own project) will connect to this Builder as a **relay over the tailnet** — not a merge. The Builder exposes a small message-in / result-out endpoint; Jess drops build requests onto the Builder's queue and reads results back. "One voice, two brains": Neill talks to Jess, she forwards build tasks to the Builder and relays the answer. No shared substrate, no shared voice pipeline — just a fat tailnet pipe (no HIPAA constraint between these two). Build this endpoint so it's ready when Jess wants it; Jess's own scope lives in her separate spec.
   137	
   138	## 11. Out of scope (v1)
   139	
   140	Wake word / always-listening; open-mic interruption (barge-in); multi-user access; trillion-parameter local models; any Google integration; home-automation integrations. **Jess's personal-assistant capabilities are explicitly out of scope for this spec** — she is a separate app on a separate stack (§10).
     1	# Chief Command v2 — Open-Source Survey & Spec Corrections (2026-07-11)
     2	
     3	Research pass requested by Neill before any scaffolding. Four parallel research lanes:
     4	OpenClaw ecosystem, local voice stack, vendor-claim verification, gauntlet/job-queue prior art.
     5	
     6	**Headline:** roughly 70% of the spec already exists as maintained open source. The genuinely
     7	novel work is three things — durable job history, budget/quality gates enforced in the database,
     8	and the iPhone voice client. Everything else is adopt, fork, or crib.
     9	
    10	**But four factual claims in the spec are wrong or stale**, and one of them (the Anthropic credit
    11	pool) is load-bearing for a design decision that is currently solving a non-problem. Fix the spec
    12	before building.
    13	
    14	---
    15	
    16	## 1. Spec corrections (do these first)
    17	
    18	| # | Spec claim | Verdict | Action |
    19	|---|---|---|---|
    20	| 1 | Claude headless `claude -p` draws from a Max-plan **monthly agent-credit pool** (§4.4) | **WRONG** — announced, then **paused before taking effect**. Anthropic's own support article: *"We're pausing the changes… For now, nothing has changed."* `claude -p` draws on normal subscription limits. | Delete §4.4's credit-pool mechanics and the budget-cap-as-necessity framing. Keep headless usage *isolated* as forward-proofing (Anthropic says it will re-propose with notice). **Do keep** the real constraint: subscription OAuth tokens in third-party tools are prohibited (terms Feb 20 2026, billing enforcement Apr 4 2026). |
    21	| 2 | Codex subagents: **up to 8 parallel workers** (§4.1) | **WRONG** — `agents.max_threads` **defaults to 6**. The "8" looks like contamination from the Grok Build claim. (`max_depth = 1` and the `~/.codex/agents/` TOML layout are **correct**; GA March 2026 **confirmed**.) | Fix fan-out arithmetic to 6, or set `max_threads` explicitly and validate empirically. |
    22	| 3 | Codex OAuth for third-party harnesses is **"sanctioned by OpenAI"** (§4.1) | **WRONG as stated.** It *works* — it's what OpenClaw's OpenAI provider uses — but no OpenAI doc sanctions it, and OpenAI's ToS forbids sharing account credentials / programmatic extraction outside permitted surfaces. It is **tolerated, not sanctioned**. | **This is the biggest latent risk in the spec.** See §5 below. |
    23	| 4 | OpenAI realtime voice ~$0.18–0.46/min → $700–1000/mo (§5.3) | **WRONG — off by ~10×.** See **§1c**, which supersedes this row. Real figure at Neill's usage: **~$25/mo (mini), ~$76/mo (full).** The architectural half (not in the subscription; separate metered billing) is CONFIRMED. Model name stale (`-2.1`). | **The sole stated justification for the local voice stack has collapsed.** Re-litigate on true numbers. Also kill the "realtime = GPT-5.6" premise — it's a distilled family with a Sept-2024 cutoff. |
    24	| 5 | Google/Gemini excluded — ToS bans third-party OAuth (names OpenClaw), consumer CLI dead June 18 2026 (§4.5) | **CONFIRMED, both halves.** ToS names OpenClaw explicitly; mass bans were *enforced* Feb 2026; consumer access ended June 18. | No change. §4.5 is correct. |
    25	| 6 | Grok Build: xAI sanctions headless + third-party harness on SuperGrok, 8 parallel subagents in worktrees (§4.2) | **PARTLY TRUE, with a tier trap.** Headless `-p` and worktree-isolated subagents **confirmed**. But "8" is reported, not guaranteed; and the "sanction" is *inferred* from xAI shipping OAuth, not stated in ToS. **Live bug report: `xai-oauth` returns HTTP 403 for standard SuperGrok ($30/mo) — backend appears gated to SuperGrok Heavy ($300/mo)**, despite the launch announcement saying all SuperGrok. | **Cheap test, expensive assumption.** Empirically verify the OAuth path on the actual account tier before this seat goes in the plan. |
    26	| 7 | Voice STT: faster-whisper or whisper.cpp (§5.3) | **WRONG for this hardware.** faster-whisper has **no Metal support** on Apple Silicon — it silently falls back to CPU. | Use **MLX Whisper**. It's 30–40% faster than whisper.cpp *and* ships in Pipecat as `WhisperSTTServiceMLX`. Faster and less work — not a tradeoff. |
    27	
    28	---
    29	
    30	## 1b. THE BIG ONE — the orchestrator seat is wrong (raised by Neill, 2026-07-11)
    31	
    32	Neill: *"Codex is slow… it may not be a great orchestrator. Surely we can do OpenClaw with a solid
    33	subscription model at the head that can do two-way voice and isn't slow af."*
    34	
    35	**He's right, and the numbers are worse than anyone guessed.**
    36	
    37	### The measured reality
    38	
    39	| Model | Time to first token |
    40	|---|---|
    41	| **GPT-5.6-sol (high effort)** — *the spec's orchestrator* | **13.0s** |
    42	| GPT-5.6-sol (max effort) | ~150s |
    43	| Claude Fable 5 (max effort) | ~109s |
    44	| Claude Sonnet 4.6 (non-reasoning) | 1.44s |
    45	| Claude Haiku 4.5 | ~1.04s |
    46	| Grok 4.1 Fast (non-reasoning) | ~2s reported — **UNVERIFIED, load-bearing, benchmark it** |
    47	| Gemini 2.5 Flash-Lite | 0.35s (excluded by §4.5 — listed for calibration only) |
    48	
    49	**13 seconds to first token, against a 1.5s budget.** That is not a tuning problem; it's ~10× over.
    50	
    51	**The dominant variable is reasoning effort, not model family.** Every frontier model collapses to
    52	unusable TTFT at high effort, and every one of them lands ~1–1.5s at low/non-reasoning. So the
    53	question was never "which vendor" — it's "how hard is the thing you're talking to trying to think."
    54	
    55	### The design flaw
    56	
    57	§5.1 has the voice layer tool-calling "the GPT-5.6 orchestrator which holds the harness memory,
    58	subagents, and reasoning." **That routes every voice turn through a deep reasoning model** — even
    59	"what did the overnight run do?" The spec then papers over it with a canned ~300ms "on it, kicking
    60	that off" while the slow model grinds. That's a bandaid on a design problem.
    61	
    62	### The fix — split two roles the spec fused into one seat
    63	
    64	This pattern has a name: **Talker–Reasoner** (Google DeepMind, arXiv 2410.08328), framed on
    65	Kahneman's System 1 / System 2. The voice community converged on the same shape independently
    66	("Fast Talker / Slow Thinker").
    67	
    68	- **Talker / the head** — *who you talk to.* Fast, non-reasoning, excellent tool-calling. **Not
    69	  smart.** It is ears, mouth, and dispatcher. Sub-second TTFT or the voice loop feels dead.
    70	- **Reasoner / the seats** — *who does the work.* Allowed to be slow, because **you are never
    71	  waiting on it.** Deep planning and long builds are async by nature.
    72	
    73	**What makes this clean rather than clever:** OpenClaw's `sessions_spawn` is **always non-blocking**
    74	— returns `runId` + `childSessionKey` immediately, with a per-spawn `model` override. The head fires
    75	"spawn a Codex build, spawn three reviewers," gets run ids back in milliseconds, and turns straight
    76	around and talks to you. It **never blocks on the slow seat.** Status comes from the SQLite job
    77	records when asked. The router-dispatches-to-subagents shape is already a documented OpenClaw idiom
    78	(reported 80–90% cost reduction vs. running the big model for everything).
    79	
    80	**So Codex being slow is fine. Codex being the thing you *talk to* is not.** Demote it from "the brain
    81	you converse with" to "a seat you dispatch to" and its latency stops mattering entirely.
    82	
    83	### ⚠️ The landmine — this kills the Claude Max head, and possibly the whole subscription premise
    84	
    85	**CLI-based runtimes cannot be in a per-turn voice loop:**
    86	
    87	- `claude -p` subprocess: **3–5s overhead per call.** Cited discussion says this makes "use cases
    88	  like voice assistants **prohibitively slow**."
    89	- Claude Agent SDK `query()`: **~12s overhead per call**, no hot-process reuse
    90	  ([claude-agent-sdk-typescript#34](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-agent-sdk-typescript/issues/34)).
    91	- With process pre-warming/reuse: drops to <1s. Without it, you eat the full cost **every turn**.
    92	- Direct Messages API: 1–3s end-to-end.
    93	
    94	This collides head-on with the constraint from §1: **`agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` is the only
    95	*legal* way to use the Claude Max subscription (Anthropic bans subscription OAuth in third-party
    96	tools) — and it is exactly the path that costs 3–5s per turn.**
    97	
    98	> **You cannot have both "Claude Max subscription" and "snappy voice head." They are mutually
    99	> exclusive.** Claude-via-CLI is fine as a *dispatch target* (4s startup on a 90-second background
   100	> job is noise). It cannot be the ears and mouth.
   101	
   102	**And the broader implication Neill has not priced in:** ChatGPT/Codex and SuperGrok are *chat/CLI
   103	subscriptions, not API credit.* The head needs a **direct streaming API path** to hit 1.5s — which
   104	likely means **the head seat requires pay-as-you-go API billing regardless of which subscription he
   105	holds.** The saving grace: the head is a non-reasoning dispatcher on short turns — the *cheapest
   106	possible seat*. Likely **dollars a month, not a cost problem.** But it must be confirmed before the
   107	architecture is committed, because "everything runs on flat-rate subscriptions" is currently an
   108	unstated premise of the whole spec, and it does not survive contact with the latency budget.
   109	
   110	The economics still work, and arguably work *better*: **the expensive thinking runs on flat-rate
   111	subscription seats; only the tiny dispatcher head touches metered API.**
   112	
   113	### Recommended seating
   114	
   115	- **Head (Talker):** in preference order — (1) **Grok 4.1 Fast Non-Reasoning** (purpose-built for
   116	  agentic tool-calling, no CoT overhead, subscription already held — *but its TTFT is unverified and
   117	  load-bearing*); (2) **Claude Haiku 4.5** via direct API (~1.04s, best TTFT consistency — needs API
   118	  billing, not the Max sub); (3) **GPT-5.6 at `none`/`low` effort** (OpenAI's own guidance names
   119	  `low` for latency-sensitive work — but same family as the 13s number, so verify).
   120	- **Explicitly NOT the head:** GPT-5.6-sol at high/max, Claude Fable, **anything behind `claude-cli`.**
   121	- **Dispatch targets (Reasoners):** GPT-5.6-sol high/max via Codex; Claude Opus/Fable via
   122	  `claude-cli`; Grok Build; Ollama. All spawned non-blocking via `sessions_spawn` with explicit
   123	  per-spawn `model`. Their multi-second startup is irrelevant in the background.
   124	
   125	### 🔬 Spike C (new, and it gates the architecture)
   126	
   127	**A 20-turn TTFT bake-off of the three head candidates**, on a representative short conversational
   128	turn *that includes a tool call in the response*, measured **from the OpenClaw agent boundary** (not
   129	the raw provider endpoint) so harness overhead is included.
   130	
   131	The published numbers are directionally right, but **the Grok figure is the load-bearing unknown,
   132	and the head-model choice is the one decision here that is expensive to reverse.** Run this before
   133	writing the seating into the spec.
   134	
   135	---
   136	
   137	## 1c. OpenAI Realtime as the voice head — the cost argument has collapsed (2026-07-11)
   138	
   139	Neill: *"OpenAI released a new live voice thing… it's expensive af, ~$20-30/hr… but they have a lite
   140	that's cheaper. This would take out the voice stack and bring latency down."*
   141	
   142	**Verdict: the architecture is right and *sanctioned*, the cost fear is unfounded in both
   143	directions — but there's a hard external blocker. Decide by spike, not by argument.**
   144	
   145	### The cost math, corrected
   146	
   147	**Everyone's numbers were wrong, including the spec's.**
   148	
   149	| Estimate | Verdict |
   150	|---|---|
   151	| Neill's ~$20–30/hr | **Wrong by ~15–20×.** Even worst-case uncached *continuous call* density is ~$7.50/hr. |
   152	| Spec §5.3's $700–1000/mo (and "$150–250 even for mini") | **Wrong by ~10×.** |
   153	| The $0.04–0.10/min benchmark | *Correct, but for the wrong workload* — that's a call-center rate (two people talking nonstop). |
   154	
   155	**The mechanic everyone missed: you are billed per audio token, not per minute of open session.**
   156	OpenAI does not charge for connection time. With push-to-talk, audio streams *only while the button
   157	is held*. **Silence, thinking time, and reading the text log cost $0.** The spec's error was billing
   158	every wall-clock minute of a 2-hour session as a fully-saturated conversational minute. A real
   159	builder session is maybe **15–25% audio-active**.
   160	
   161	**Modeled at Neill's actual pattern** (2 hrs/day, PTT, short spoken summaries, cached prompt prefix):
   162	
   163	| | gpt-realtime-2.1 | **gpt-realtime-2.1-mini** |
   164	|---|---|---|
   165	| Per hour | ~$1.27 | **~$0.42** |
   166	| **Per month (60 hrs)** | **~$76** | **~$25** |
   167	| Chattier sensitivity case | ~$167/mo | ~$59/mo |
   168	
   169	**~$25/mo is not a real cost.** The local-stack decision was made on math that was off by an order of
   170	magnitude, and **deserves to be re-litigated on true numbers** — whatever we end up choosing.
   171	
   172	### ❌ Kill the "realtime = GPT-5.6" premise
   173	
   174	It isn't. `gpt-realtime-2.1` and `-mini` are a **separate distilled family** with a **Sept 30, 2024
   175	knowledge cutoff** and 128k context. GPT-5.6-sol has a **Feb 2026 cutoff** and 1.05M context. They
   176	are 17 months and an order of magnitude of context apart. Mini is explicitly *"a distilled reasoning
   177	model for faster, lower-cost realtime voice."* The configurable reasoning-effort knob is probably
   178	what created the "it's 5.6" impression.
   179	
   180	**Owner-level correction:** §5.1's emotional core — *"you are talking to your actual orchestrator"* —
   181	is **false under BOTH architectures.** Either way, the head is a small fast model that dispatches to
   182	the real brain. That's fine (see §1b: it's the *right* design), but the spec should stop promising
   183	otherwise.
   184	
   185	### ✅ OpenClaw already has the exact pattern — natively
   186	
   187	The **voice-call plugin** bundles OpenAI as a realtime provider and exposes an
   188	**`openclaw_agent_consult`** tool by default: the realtime model calls **back into the full OpenClaw
   189	agent** for deeper reasoning and normal OpenClaw tools. **OpenClaw keeps ownership of tool execution
   190	and harness context; the realtime model is purely the conversational shell.**
   191	
   192	That is Talker–Reasoner, natively, as a sanctioned design. The hypothesis isn't just sound — it's
   193	the documented pattern. It also directly answers Neill's *"I need deep reasoning when coming up with
   194	ideas"*: `openclaw_agent_consult` **is** the escalation path from the fast mouth to the slow brain.
   195	
   196	### 🚨 The blocker — and it's someone else's open bug
   197	
   198	**[OpenClaw #80196](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/80196), open since May 10 2026,
   199	unresolved:** OpenClaw's realtime voice provider is **hardcoded to OpenAI's beta protocol.** Pointed
   200	at a GA model it fails with `Missing required parameter: 'session.type'`. Two gates: the
   201	`OpenAI-Beta: realtime=v1` header, and a missing GA session schema in the transport layer.
   202	
   203	**So `gpt-realtime-2.1` / `-mini` are NOT usable through OpenClaw today without patching.**
   204	`gpt-realtime-1.5` (beta) works. Workarounds: brittle manual patches, stay on the beta model, or
   205	**build a standalone Python bridge outside OpenClaw** — which is *exactly the hand-wired-audio-
   206	plumbing trap that killed v1.* (Related open issues: #90456, #5606, #13245, #71195.)
   207	
   208	### Other things that bite
   209	
   210	- **Hard 60-minute session cap.** A 2-hour builder session needs ≥2 reconnects with context
   211	  rehydration — and a fresh session means a **cold cache** (cost spike + a memory seam). Needs
   212	  design, not hand-waving.
   213	- **Prompt caching is load-bearing, not an optimization.** Without it the context-replay term goes
   214	  from $0.168/hr → **$13.44/hr**. That is the entire difference between "cheap" and "unusable."
   215	  Requires a stable prefix — never interpolate timestamps into the system prompt.
   216	- **Adopting realtime does NOT remove Pipecat** (if we go the Pipecat route). It removes **Whisper
   217	  and Kokoro**. Pipecat supports realtime as a first-class service (`OpenAIRealtimeLLMService`).
   218	- **Tool calling is good, not great.** Slightly below text-model accuracy; harder to debug (the text
   219	  intermediate is implicit); a tool round-trip adds **400–800ms**. The real failure mode: the model
   220	  fires a tool call then goes **silent**, the user assumes it dropped and interrupts, corrupting

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md | sed -n '221,460p'" in /Users/user/code-projects/chief-command
 succeeded in 0ms:
   221	  state. Mitigation is *spoken preambles* — **which §5.1 already invented independently** ("on it,
   222	  kicking that off").
   223	- ⚠️ **Cautionary:** OpenClaw's voice-call plugin currently uses realtime for **transcription only**,
   224	  and clocks **5–10s latency** because generation still routes through the embedded agent.
   225	  STT-only realtime does **not** solve the head-latency problem.
   226	- Realtime needs a **Platform API key** — metered, separate from the ChatGPT/Codex subscription.
   227	  (Spec already got this right.)
   228	
   229	### The honest case for staying local
   230	
   231	$0 forever · no 60-min cap · no vendor drift (**§9's named #1 risk — and a metered Platform API is
   232	exactly the surface OpenAI is most likely to reprice**) · offline · no audio leaves the machine.
   233	
   234	**The honest case against:** hand-wiring streaming audio **is what killed v1**, and the paid path now
   235	costs ~$25/mo rather than the $150–250 the spec assumed.
   236	
   237	### 🔬 Spike D (new) — settle this empirically, ~1 day
   238	
   239	Two questions, both cheap, and together they decide the whole voice architecture:
   240	1. **Does OpenClaw's realtime provider work against `gpt-realtime-2.1-mini`, or does #80196 bite?**
   241	2. **Is prior assistant audio retained in the replayed context as *audio* (expensive) or as *text
   242	   transcript* (~16× cheaper)?** Docs don't say. Run a 30-min session and read the dashboard.
   243	   (latent.space's advice to "replace audio messages with text messages" implies audio *is* retained
   244	   by default — if so, my cost model above is optimistic.)
   245	
   246	**Do not rewrite the spec until these are answered.** The cost argument that drove the original
   247	local-stack decision has collapsed; the remaining blocker is technical and external.
   248	
   249	---
   250	
   251	## 1d. 🔴 OWNER OVERRIDE — push-to-talk is dead (Neill, 2026-07-11)
   252	
   253	> *"I kinda want you to be one as didn't work. It was fucked. I don't want push to talk. I wanna be
   254	> able to just talk back and forth, two way voice. Fuck push to talk."*
   255	>
   256	> *"I just wanna be driving in my car and being able to talk to my builders."*
   257	
   258	**This overrides the spec. §5.2 ("Push-to-talk on all clients… No wake word, no open mic in v1") and
   259	§11 (wake word = out of scope) are REVOKED by the owner.**
   260	
   261	New hard requirement, at the level of §Prime-directive:
   262	
   263	- **Hands-free, continuous, two-way voice.** No button. Barge-in expected.
   264	- **Must work while driving** — phone in a car, hands on the wheel.
   265	- This is a *product* requirement, not a phase-3 nicety. **v1 died on a bad voice experience; a
   266	  version that requires holding a button is, in the owner's judgment, the same failure.**
   267	
   268	### What this changes, and what it doesn't
   269	
   270	**Doesn't change:** open-mic/VAD turn detection is the OpenAI Realtime API's **default**;
   271	`turn_detection: null` (push-to-talk) is the **opt-out**. Barge-in/interruption is native. So
   272	hands-free is *strictly less work* than PTT on the realtime path. **The voice backend is not the
   273	constraint.**
   274	
   275	**Does change — and this is the real cost:**
   276	1. **The PTT cost model in §1c is now invalid.** PTT's "you don't pay for silence" saving is gone if
   277	   the mic streams continuously. **Mitigation: local VAD gating on the device** — only open the
   278	   stream when speech is actually detected. Needs sizing (see Spike E).
   279	2. **§9's "iOS PWA audio quirks" risk is now the critical path, not a phase-3 footnote.** A PWA
   280	   very likely **cannot** hold a background mic or run wake-word detection with the screen locked.
   281	   **This may force a native iOS app** — a scope change the spec does not contemplate anywhere.
   282	3. **CarPlay entitlements are gated by Apple to specific app categories** and may be a hard blocker
   283	   for a custom personal app. Needs verification.
   284	4. **Wake word may not even be needed** for the car case: phone mounted/on-screen, app in
   285	   foreground, conversation simply stays open for the drive — like a phone call. Wake word is only
   286	   required for *locked-phone-in-pocket* invocation. **Scope the car case first; it's much cheaper.**
   287	
   288	### ✅ RESOLVED — hands-free is achievable, and cheaper than PTT
   289	
   290	**Answer to the owner's literal question — *"can I only get that if I build my own voice stack?"* —
   291	NO. Building our own stack gets us NOTHING here. The Realtime API does open-mic hands-free out of
   292	the box, better and with less work.**
   293	
   294	**The hands-free constraint is 100% an iOS/client problem, entirely orthogonal to the voice
   295	backend.** Realtime vs. local-stack changes latency, cost, and privacy — it changes **nothing**
   296	about whether the mic can be open while driving. Pick the backend on other merits; solve hands-free
   297	separately.
   298	
   299	#### Open mic is the DEFAULT (confirmed from docs)
   300	
   301	- `turn_detection` **defaults to `server_vad`**. **`turn_detection: null` IS push-to-talk** — the
   302	  opt-out. **Reverting §5.2 is a deletion, not a build.**
   303	- **Use `semantic_vad` with `eagerness: "low"`** — a classifier that judges whether the speaker
   304	  actually *sounded finished*. This is the right call for a car: **a driver pauses mid-sentence to
   305	  merge, and plain `server_vad` will cut him off.** Semantic VAD is specifically designed not to.
   306	- **Barge-in is native** — `interrupt_response: true` + `create_response: true`. User speech during
   307	  assistant playback truncates the assistant's turn.
   308	
   309	#### Open-mic cost: a non-issue
   310	
   311	Audio is duration-encoded: user audio = **600 tok/min**. Worst case, if silence were billed raw:
   312	**$0.36/hr (mini)** / $1.15/hr (full). A 45-min drive = **$0.27 on mini.** Rounding error next to the
   313	conversation itself.
   314	
   315	Community consensus (medium confidence, forum-sourced, *not* a documented billing guarantee) is that
   316	**uncommitted silence is not billed at all** under server VAD, since non-speech is discarded and
   317	never committed as an input item. **Don't architect around this — measure it** (15-min experiment:
   318	stream silence, read `response.usage`). Local-VAD gating is available if needed (Pipecat ships
   319	`SileroVADAnalyzer`; set `turn_detection=False` and audio never leaves the device during silence) —
   320	but at $0.36/hr worst case, **don't build it preemptively.** The real cost driver in long sessions is
   321	context replay, not silence.
   322	
   323	#### 🚨 iOS: one hard NO, one hard blocker, and a sleeper
   324	
   325	| Path | Verdict |
   326	|---|---|
   327	| **PWA, background / locked screen, wake word** | 🔴 **IMPOSSIBLE.** Not hard — *impossible.* iOS suspends PWAs shortly after backgrounding; service workers get only narrow event-driven windows and **cannot** do continuous audio ([WebKit #211018](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=211018)). State this to the owner without hedging. |
   328	| **PWA, foreground, phone mounted, open mic** | ✅ **WORKS TODAY. This is the 90% answer.** Tap once on entering the car, then talk freely the whole drive, with barge-in. Add **Wake Lock API** to keep the screen awake. ~**a day of work, mostly deleting PTT logic.** Gotcha: use a single-page shell with **no hash routing** — iOS standalone-mode `getUserMedia` re-prompts on navigation ([WebKit #185448](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=185448), [#215884](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=215884)). |
   329	| **Siri App Intent as launcher → hands off to our session** | ⭐ **THE SLEEPER. Best effort-to-payoff ratio.** *"Hey Siri, ask Chief…"* works **hands-free, screen locked, phone in pocket**, needs **no special entitlement and no wake-word engine** — Siri gives us the system-level wake word for free, then our app takes over the mic and runs the full open-mic Realtime session. Requires a thin native shell. Ceiling (inferred): Siri is a *launcher*, not a host — you don't run a Realtime session inside Siri's voice loop. |
   330	| **Native app + Porcupine + `UIBackgroundModes: audio`** | ✅ Achievable. True custom *"hey Chief"*, screen off, in-pocket. **Sideloading removes the App Store constraint entirely** — `UIBackgroundModes: audio` is a *normal capability*, not a restricted entitlement. No review, no rejection risk. Costs: a real native app, a wake-word integration, and a **persistent red mic dot in the status bar** (cannot be suppressed). |
   331	| **CarPlay** | 🔴 **HARD BLOCKER. CUT IT.** iOS 26.4 *did* add exactly the right category (voice-based conversational apps, built for ChatGPT/Gemini-class assistants) — but **CarPlay entitlements are Apple-granted per-category, case-by-case, and cannot be self-signed.** No personal provisioning profile can carry one. Apple granting a one-man internal tool a conversational-AI CarPlay entitlement is ~zero. Also: Apple is explicitly *not* handing the wake-word slot to third parties — Siri stays the system assistant in CarPlay. |
   332	
   333	#### 🔧 Owner data point: the Archie app (real prior experience, and it corrects the above)
   334	
   335	Neill: *"We got a native iOS app for Archie, the app still has to be open. And we haven't gotten it
   336	to work yet."*
   337	
   338	**My hypothesis was that a WebView-wrapped app can never get background mic. That is WRONG — and the
   339	truth is better news.**
   340	
   341	- **[WebKit #233419](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=233419) is RESOLVED CONFIGURATION
   342	  CHANGED (Oct 2024).** A WKWebView's `getUserMedia` **does** survive backgrounding — **if the host
   343	  app's Info.plist declares `UIBackgroundModes: audio`.** Reporter confirmation on iOS 17.5.1:
   344	  *"Without adding 'audio' to the UIBackgroundModes it didn't work, but with that setting the
   345	  microphone works in the WKWebView while the app is in the background."*
   346	- **Archie's symptom is the textbook signature of that flag being missing from the BUILT app.**
   347	  Capacitor/Cordova regenerate Info.plist and routinely drop it. **10-minute test: unzip the built
   348	  `.app`, check its Info.plist for `UIBackgroundModes = [audio]` — not the source plist, the built
   349	  one.** This may fix Archie outright.
   350	- **But do NOT build Chief on WebView background mic.** It works, but it's WebKit behavior, not an
   351	  Apple API contract — undocumented, and it regressed across iOS 14/15/16 (three separate bugs). You
   352	  get **no control over the AVAudioSession category, no interruption handling, no route-change
   353	  handling, and no ability to reactivate after a phone call.** Every failure mode below becomes
   354	  unfixable by you.
   355	
   356	#### 🔴 The hard truth: always-on wake-word-in-pocket is NOT a solved problem
   357	
   358	**Do not promise this. The evidence says iOS reclaims it for a meaningful fraction of users — even
   359	in fully native apps.**
   360	
   361	- Apple's contract is conditional: the app keeps running in background *"as long as it is recording
   362	  audio content."* An idle session gets suspended.
   363	- **[Apple Forums #776055](https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/776055):** a *shipping* native
   364	  background-recording app reports a subset of users **routinely get recordings killed** by the
   365	  watchdog. They stripped the entire view hierarchy on background to get CPU to 0% in the profiler,
   366	  saw "massive improvements," and **still see background terminations.** Apple publishes **no CPU or
   367	  memory thresholds** for what survives.
   368	- **Picovoice's own FAQ hedges:** background wake-word "is controlled by the operating system, and
   369	  Picovoice **cannot guarantee** that this will be possible in future releases of iOS."
   370	- Every phone call, Siri invocation, or app grabbing the mic is an interruption you must catch and
   371	  recover from — and recovery is not always granted. User swipes the app away → dead, nothing
   372	  relaunches it. Plus a permanent orange mic indicator and real battery drain.
   373	- **Shazam's auto-detect is NOT a third-party pattern** — it's an OS feature. Can't be replicated.
   374	- Otter/voice-recorders are **background *recording*** (user explicitly started it, knows it's
   375	  running) — **not always-on ambient listening.** Different thing.
   376	
   377	**Neill has already run this experiment once and lost. Don't send him back in.**
   378	
   379	#### ⚠️ Siri's real ceiling (corrects the "sleeper" call above)
   380	
   381	Siri's wake word runs on the OS's always-on low-power processor — **your app needs zero background
   382	mic and isn't running at all until invoked.** That part is confirmed and it's the whole appeal. But:
   383	
   384	- **Background intent** (`openAppWhenRun = false`) + `authenticationPolicy = .alwaysAllowed` → **works
   385	  fully hands-free, phone locked, in pocket.** Siri speaks the result. ✅
   386	- **Foreground intent** (`openAppWhenRun = true`) on a **locked** device → **prompts the user to
   387	  unlock, and the shortcut is interrupted.** Siri does not dismiss the lock screen. ❌
   388	- **So "Hey Siri, ask Chief" cannot launch straight into a live open-mic conversation on a locked
   389	  phone.** It can *answer a question* hands-free while locked. To enter a conversation, you unlock
   390	  (a Face ID glance — a gate, but a small one).
   391	- Uncertainty flagged: some devs report `.alwaysAllowed` still prompting in certain configs. **Device
   392	  test, not a spec guarantee.**
   393	
   394	#### Wake-word engines (only needed for the pocket case)
   395	
   396	**Porcupine** (free tier covers personal use; ~$6K/yr only bites on commercial distribution) — native
   397	iOS + macOS SDKs, type-to-train custom words, **97.1% detection at 10 dB SNR on a noise dataset that
   398	includes car/traffic noise.** **openWakeWord** (Apache-2.0, free) has no first-party iOS SDK — you own
   399	the ONNX/TFLite port.
   400	
   401	⚠️ **Word choice matters more than engine choice.** *"Hey Chief"* is a **weak** wake word — short,
   402	common phoneme, easily false-triggered by the word "chief" in speech and by radio/podcast audio.
   403	Use a longer, rarer phrase: **"hey chief command"** or **"okay chief."**
   404	
   405	### Recommended path (revised after the Archie data point)
   406	
   407	1. **Foreground PWA, mounted phone, open mic (`semantic_vad`, eagerness low, barge-in on).** The 90%
   408	   answer, ~a day, no native code, **no background audio needed at all** — which is precisely why it
   409	   sidesteps the wall Archie hit. **Do this now.**
   410	2. **Siri background App Intent** (`.alwaysAllowed`) → *"Hey Siri, ask Chief what the overnight run
   411	   did"* answers hands-free, locked, in-pocket, with **zero background mic.** Doesn't open a
   412	   conversation — answers a question. **Spec as the follow-on; it covers most pocket use.**
   413	3. **Native capture layer** (`AVAudioSession(.playAndRecord)` + `AVAudioEngine` tap + interruption/
   414	   route-change handling) **if and only if** we need screen-off long sessions. Model on
   415	   `@capgo/capacitor-audio-recorder` (MIT). Note: **no maintained Capacitor plugin does background
   416	   wake-word** — that's custom native (Porcupine SDK + own bridge).
   417	4. **Always-on custom "hey Chief" from a sleeping pocket — DO NOT PROMISE.** Achievable-ish, not
   418	   reliable. Revisit only if #1 and #2 genuinely don't cover the need.
   419	5. **CarPlay — cut from the roadmap.** Entitlement is Apple-granted and unsignable.
   420	
   421	**Cheap immediate win for the owner, unrelated to Chief:** check Archie's **built** `.app` Info.plist
   422	for `UIBackgroundModes: audio`. If missing, that one line may be the whole bug.
   423	
   424	---
   425	
   426	## 2. What already exists (don't rebuild it)
   427	
   428	### OpenClaw core covers ~80% of the harness
   429	
   430	- **The four seats** = OpenClaw's `agents.list[]`. Each entry gets its own `model` (+ `fallbacks`),
   431	  `workspace`, `tools` profile, `identity`. All four providers natively supported:
   432	  - **Codex** — supported, but a distinct runtime path (`codex` plugin + native app-server harness). Fiddliest of the four; budget real time.
   433	  - **Grok/xAI** — supported; SuperGrok OAuth is the documented recommended path (subject to the tier trap above).
   434	  - **Ollama** — supported, auto-detected at `127.0.0.1:11434`.
   435	  - **Claude headless** — supported via `agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` (reuses `claude -p` under your own session auth). This is the escalation seat.
   436	- **The dispatcher** = native subagents (`sessions_spawn`). Non-blocking, returns a run id,
   437	  and **each spawn takes an explicit `model` param** — so one orchestrator turn fans out across
   438	  Grok + Ollama + Claude simultaneously. `maxConcurrent` default 8; `maxSpawnDepth` 2 is the
   439	  documented orchestrator pattern. **Do not build a custom dispatcher.**
   440	- **The reviewer gauntlet** = the stock `autoreview` skill. Runs multiple reviewers against one
   441	  **frozen bundle** (identical input — this matters), with per-reviewer model and reasoning-effort
   442	  overrides: `autoreview --reviewers codex,claude --model codex=gpt-5.6-sol --thinking codex=high`.
   443	  That is most of §6, for free.
   444	- **Tailnet + launchd** = Gateway binds loopback:18789, **auto-configures Tailscale Serve**,
   445	  serves the Control UI on the same port; `openclaw gateway install` writes the launchd service.
   446	  §2's networking and daemon requirements are shipped features.
   447	
   448	**Version pinning is not optional.** Three breaking releases in under 90 days in Q1 2026
   449	(2026.3.2, 2026.3.8, 2026.3.13). Exact pins are honored and not overwritten by the updater.
   450	This is precisely the vendor-drift risk §9 names — pin an exact version, upgrade deliberately.
   451	
   452	### The voice server half is already built
   453	
   454	**`kwindla/macos-local-voice-agents`** — reference implementation by Pipecat's originator
   455	(Kwindla Kramer, Daily CEO). Stack: Silero VAD · smart-turn v2 · **MLX Whisper · Kokoro TTS** ·
   456	WebRTC · React client. Claims **<800ms voice-to-voice on M-series** *with a local LLM in the loop*.
   457	Our budget is 1.5s to first audio with the orchestrator doing the thinking — real headroom.
   458	⚠️ Only 17 commits and **LICENSE unverified — check before forking.** Treat as demo-grade code
   459	to learn from, not a maintained dependency.
   460	

