   461	**Pipecat** (BSD-2, v1.0 April 2026) is the framework. Critically, **push-to-talk is an in-tree
   462	abstraction** — `PushToTalkUserTurnStrategies` (see `pipecat-examples/push-to-talk`): client sends
   463	`{type:"push_to_talk", state:"start"/"stop"}`, server-side aggregator collects transcription only
   464	during the hold and discards anything outside the window. **We do not hand-wire turn-taking.**
   465	That is the exact failure mode that killed v1 (§5.3: *"Chief Command v1 died hand-wiring this"*).
   466	
   467	### Gauntlet: someone already proved the riskiest assumption
   468	
   469	**AdamsReview** (MIT, 240★, active) dispatches **up to 7 parallel sub-agent lenses** and — the key
   470	finding — **runs on plain Claude Code subscription session auth, no API keys**. Its `/codex-review`
   471	shells out to **Codex CLI as a genuine cross-family peer**, and `/review --ensemble` merges Codex
   472	findings with the Claude lenses. That is §6's "at least two model families, on subscription seats"
   473	rule *working in public today*. It was the part of the spec I'd have bet against.
   474	
   475	Its `/walkthrough` (interactive human review of uncertain findings) is the model for the human
   476	escalation queue; `/promote` escalates a finding past a gate.
   477	
   478	**Also worth cribbing:**
   479	- **open-code-review** (Apache-2.0) — 8-phase Tech Lead orchestration with a **Discourse phase**
   480	  (reviewers challenge each other *before* synthesis) — the disagreement-surfacing mechanism §6
   481	  wants before escalation. Per-reviewer model assignment is first-class. Persists to SQLite.
   482	- **agent-review-panel** — **blind independent scoring before debate** (anti-groupthink), plus a
   483	  post-judge gate that re-verifies any P0/P1 the judge itself introduced (catches judge
   484	  hallucination). Note: it hard-pins every subagent to one model and manufactures diversity via
   485	  personas — the *opposite* of our premise. Take the protocol, discard the model policy. Blind
   486	  scoring works *better* for us, since our reviewers are genuinely different models.
   487	- **ai-code-reviewer** (calimero) — verdict aggregation formula: cluster findings, weight by **how
   488	  many agents independently agree**, rank by severity × agreement, stop when findings converge.
   489	
   490	### Job queue: ORCH is the closest whole system
   491	
   492	**ORCH** (MIT, 560 commits, v1.0.27 July 2026, 1,954 passing tests):
   493	- State machine **`todo → in_progress → review → done`** — the `review` state is a first-class gate,
   494	  i.e. a ready-made gauntlet hook.
   495	- **8 CLI adapters: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Pi, OpenCode, Grok, Antigravity, and Shell.**
   496	  The generic Shell adapter covers Ollama and anything else. **This adapter layer is the single
   497	  most reusable artifact in the entire survey.**
   498	- **Every agent gets an isolated git worktree on its own branch; `main` untouched until approved.**
   499	  That's §7's git-as-audit-trail, already built.
   500	- Exponential-backoff retry + **zombie-task detection with re-queue** (matters a lot for unattended
   501	  overnight runs).
   502	- ❌ **The mismatch:** ORCH's design stance is *"zero infrastructure — no database."* State is
   503	  YAML/JSON/JSONL in `.orchestry/`. That directly contradicts §7's queryable build history.
   504	  Fork the adapters + state machine; replace the persistence.
   505	
   506	---
   507	
   508	## 3. What we actually build (the real scope)
   509	
   510	The survey's central finding: **the spec sits in a genuine gap.** Every project with a good
   511	reviewer panel is either single-model-family or API-key-billed. Every project with a good job queue
   512	either has no database (ORCH) or doesn't dispatch (clu). **Nothing does gauntlet + queryable
   513	history + subscription-seat auth together.** That gap is the thing worth building — and it's much
   514	narrower than the spec assumes.
   515	
   516	1. **The SQLite job/history schema.** Nobody hands you this. It's §7's queryable build history
   517	   ("what did the overnight run do?"). ~A day of work, not a dependency. Read **clu**'s schema
   518	   first — specifically its **atomic task claiming via `UPDATE … RETURNING` with a subquery**, which
   519	   solves parallel-reviewer race conditions for free — and open-code-review's tables second.
   520	   Note OpenClaw's subagent sessions **auto-archive after 60 min and get soft-deleted** — that's
   521	   session archival, *not* durable job records. This layer is genuinely missing.
   522	
   523	2. **Enforce the quality/budget rules in the database, not in prompts.** §5.3's *"local output never
   524	   ships without a subscription-tier review"* must be a **transition guard + schema constraint**: a
   525	   job whose builder was the Ollama seat is *structurally unable* to reach `done` without a
   526	   subscription-tier verdict row. §9 already says to enforce this "in the pipeline itself, not by
   527	   convention" — this is what that means concretely. **Rules that live in prompts quietly stop
   528	   holding at 4am.** Same for per-seat spend caps: OpenClaw has **no per-agent cost/token budget
   529	   anywhere in core**, so this is ours regardless.
   530	
   531	3. **The iPhone PWA voice client.** Nobody has shipped an open-source streaming-Whisper + Kokoro +
   532	   iOS-PWA push-to-talk assistant. The server half is solved prior art; **this half we write.**
   533	
   534	---
   535	
   536	## 4. Spikes to run before writing client code
   537	
   538	**Spike C (§1b — the head-model TTFT bake-off) is the highest priority of the three**, because it
   539	gates the architecture rather than the client. A and B below gate the client. All are cheap; all
   540	have clean fallbacks. §9 guessed iOS audio would be the pain — it turns out to be two specific,
   541	testable questions rather than a swamp.
   542	
   543	- **Spike A — WebRTC over Tailscale, iPhone Safari → Mac.** No STUN/TURN on a tailnet; `aiortc`
   544	  should negotiate host candidates directly over the Tailscale interface, but **no prior art
   545	  confirms Pipecat SmallWebRTC over Tailscale specifically**, and this is the likeliest thing to
   546	  break. **Fallback: Pipecat's WebSocket transport** — over a tailnet, WebSocket audio is entirely
   547	  viable and sidesteps all of ICE. Honestly, consider *starting* there.
   548	- **Spike B — `getUserMedia` in *installed standalone PWA* mode on the actual iPhone.** Long WebKit
   549	  bug history (#185448, #252465), status in 2026 is "mostly fixed but verify."
   550	  **Fallback: ship as a Safari tab** rather than an installed PWA.
   551	
   552	Other confirmed iOS landmines: never call `getUserMedia` twice (the second call mutes the first
   553	stream's tracks and you **cannot** programmatically unmute them — call once, keep a global stream,
   554	use `MediaStream.clone()`); `playsinline` is mandatory; device IDs are randomized per page load, so
   555	don't persist them. Audio playback needs a user gesture — but push-to-talk *is* one, so unlock the
   556	`AudioContext` on first button press.
   557	
   558	---
   559	
   560	## 5. The strategic risk to put in front of Neill
   561	
   562	**The spec's reliance on Codex third-party OAuth is the biggest latent danger in the document.**
   563	
   564	It works today. But OpenAI has not sanctioned it, its ToS forbids credential sharing, and **both
   565	other major vendors have closed this exact door in the last five months**:
   566	
   567	- **Anthropic** — terms updated Feb 20 2026 prohibiting subscription OAuth in third-party tools;
   568	  billing enforcement live Apr 4 2026.
   569	- **Google** — ToS names OpenClaw explicitly; **mass account bans** from mid-Feb 2026 (some spilling
   570	  into Gmail/Workspace access), then a system-wide unban Mar 2; consumer CLI killed June 18.
   571	
   572	OpenAI is the last vendor that hasn't clamped down. **That is a timing position, not a policy
   573	position.** §9 already calls vendor policy drift the #1 risk — this is that risk, with a name.
   574	
   575	**Mitigation:** design so the Codex auth path is **swappable to a paid API key without
   576	re-architecture**. OpenClaw's provider abstraction gives us this nearly for free. Cheap insurance
   577	against being the next OpenClaw headline.
   578	
   579	---
   580	
   581	## 6. Ranked recommendations
   582	
   583	**Adopt as-is**
   584	- OpenClaw core, **pinned to an exact version** — seats as `agents.list[]`, `codex` plugin for the
   585	  orchestrator, `agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` for the Claude seat.
   586	- Native subagents for dispatch (`maxSpawnDepth: 2`, per-spawn `model` override).
   587	- `autoreview` skill as the gauntlet spine.
   588	- `openclaw gateway install` (launchd) + Tailscale Serve auto-config.
   589	- **Pipecat** (BSD-2) as the voice framework; `kokoro-onnx` via `pipecat-ai[kokoro]` for TTS;
   590	  `WhisperSTTServiceMLX` for STT.
   591	
   592	**Fork / vendor**
   593	- **`kwindla/macos-local-voice-agents`** as the voice-server skeleton (verify LICENSE first).
   594	  Three surgical changes: rip out VAD/smart-turn → `PushToTalkUserTurnStrategies`; replace the local
   595	  LLM with a `FrameProcessor` that forwards to the orchestrator and streams the reply back
   596	  sentence-by-sentence into Kokoro; replace the React desktop client with the PWA.
   597	- **ORCH's adapter layer + state machine** (not ORCH itself) — back it with our SQLite, drop
   598	  `.orchestry/`.
   599	- **Lobster + PR #20** if we want deterministic bounded review loops — **loops are not in mainline**;
   600	  the fix is an unmerged PR. Vendor at a pinned commit with #20 cherry-picked rather than waiting
   601	  on upstream.
   602	
   603	**Crib, don't depend**
   604	- AdamsReview (panel shape + subscription-CLI auth pattern + `/walkthrough` human queue).
   605	- agent-review-panel (blind-score-before-debate; post-judge verification gate).
   606	- open-code-review (Discourse phase; SQLite schema).
   607	- calimero (severity × agreement-count aggregation).
   608	- clu (SQLite atomic claiming, checkpoints).
   609	- `claude-to-codex` / `ccode-to-codex` — **take the mapping spec, hand-write the ~200 lines.** Both
   610	  are experimental (`claude-to-codex` self-describes as *"primarily vibe-coded"*, 6★) and **silently
   611	  drop hooks, `maxTurns`, and memory settings.** Our agent definitions are the actual product —
   612	  don't run an unvetted converter over them. Note the non-obvious effort remap
   613	  (`high`→`xhigh`, `medium`→`high`).
   614	
   615	**Ignore**
   616	- **vibe-kanban** — 27.3k★ and the obvious choice a year ago, but **officially sunsetting.** If any
   617	  part of the plan was implicitly anchored to it, retire that assumption now.
   618	- LiteLLM as infrastructure — it's an HTTP gateway; our seats are *spawned CLI subprocesses under
   619	  session auth*. Structural mismatch, plus its Anthropic OAuth pass-through is buggy (open issues
   620	  #29190, #13380) and ToS-gray. Optional adapter for the API-key reviewers only; never the spine.
   621	- LiveKit Agents (room model / SFU is overkill for single-user), Vocode, Claworc.
   622	
   623	**AGENTS.md note (§6):** Codex reads it natively; **Claude Code's support is *not* fully established**
   624	(open feature request anthropics/claude-code#6235). Robust pattern that works today: make
   625	**AGENTS.md the single source of truth and CLAUDE.md a one-line `@AGENTS.md` import** — don't rely
   626	on fallback behavior.
   627	
   628	---
   629	
   630	## 7. Suggested build order (unchanged in shape, cheaper than planned)
   631	
   632	Phase 1 (text harness) is now mostly *configuration* rather than construction: pinned OpenClaw +
   633	four seats in `agents.list[]` + Tailscale Serve + launchd, then the two things core doesn't give us
   634	(SQLite job records, the never-ship-unreviewed transition guard). The Phase-1 acceptance test from
   635	the spec — *"from the phone, type 'have the local model write X, then have Codex review it' and it
   636	happens, with the job recorded"* — is reachable much faster than the spec assumes.
   637	
   638	Run **Spike A and Spike B early** (they gate Phase 3's design, and the WebSocket fallback is a
   639	decision to make *before* building the client, not after). Run the **Grok tier test** before
   640	committing the §4.2 seat.

