codex
No—I do not sign off yet. The revision is substantially better, and Decision E is honestly disclosed, but three load-bearing mechanisms remain incomplete. These are design gaps, not merely tests for the build team.

## The four required changes

1. Repository admission: partly addressed

The [admission rule](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:39) asks the right questions and correctly excludes repositories whose merges have real-world effects. The EMR refusal and explicit allowlist are also the right shape.

What is missing is control of the admission process itself:

- The document does not say that only the authenticated owner can create, change, or revoke an admission record.
- It does not say the admission record and allowlist live outside every place an agent can edit.
- Admission appears permanent. Repository automation, outside integrations, secrets, auto-pulling machines, and publishing settings can change later.
- An admitted repository can itself receive a change that adds publishing or deployment behavior. That must cancel its admission before the change can be pushed or merged.
- Repository identity should use GitHub’s permanent identity, not merely a name that can be renamed or reused.

There is also a direct sequencing conflict. The admission rule says no automation may run agent-written code before the gauntlet finishes. But the [broker section](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:154) contemplates pushing a branch—and therefore potentially starting automation—before the gauntlet finishes, provided that automation lacks valuable credentials or production effects. Those are different rules.

The exact reviewed version must pass the gauntlet before its first push, or the admission rule must be weakened honestly. Round 7 required the former.

Finally, the “owner’s tap becomes the manual deploy step” exception is safe only if that tap physically starts the separate deployment. It cannot rescue a repository where merging itself deploys or where the repository is already the live product.

2. Connected-tool exclusions: mostly addressed, but one broker claim fails

The blanket prohibition on production credentials, patient data, publishing authority, live-data access, and administrative authority is now clear and appropriately firm.

Most of the GitHub restrictions are feasible: GitHub can withhold repository administration, package, deployment, and branch-rule permissions, and it can restrict an app to selected repositories.

But this sentence does not yet deliver:

> The broker is unable to create releases.

GitHub’s permission used for writing repository contents is also sufficient to create a release. GitHub documents both facts: HTTP-based Git access requires repository-content permission, while creating a release also accepts that same permission. That permission can also send certain repository events that start automation. A narrow token alone therefore cannot make the release prohibition true. [GitHub App permission guide](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/registering-a-github-app/choosing-permissions-for-a-github-app), [GitHub release permissions](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/releases?apiVersion=2026-03-10), [complete permission mapping](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/authentication/permissions-required-for-github-apps?apiVersion=2026-03-10).

The broker needs an independently enforced outer filter that permits only the exact push, pull-request, and merge operations. That filter must remain effective even if the broker program is fooled or compromised. Alternatively, the document must admit that the credential retains release authority—but that would broaden the accepted blast radius.

3. Corrected claims and benchmark: genuinely addressed

This change passes.

The document now correctly distinguishes source reversal from consequence reversal, describes the harness as safer in blast radius but more autonomous, gives no credit for the unmigrated live record, admits that reviews are not yet tied to the exact version, and presents the expanded residual risks plainly.

My spot-check of the current harness supports that honesty:

- The admission system and GitHub broker are not built yet.
- The live record still lacks the source version’s protection against deleting verdicts.
- The one item marked finished still lacks its run and branch identity.
- The provider credential and old direct execution paths still exist.

Those are now presented as unfinished build and decommissioning work, not as completed protection.

Two sentences still need correction:

- The [one-sentence summary](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:78) says “real money” is behind a door agents cannot open. Agents are explicitly allowed to spend routine amounts. It should say spending beyond fixed limits is behind the door.
- “The extra autonomy risk lands almost entirely” on merges and spend is too narrow. Continuous audio exposure, unattended queue flooding, and extra disclosure to providers are also new risks, even though they are named later.

4. Decision E: honestly recorded, conditional on fixing admission

[Decision E](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:208) accurately records the choice offered and the owner’s decision. It does not pretend voice recognition proves intent.

Hands-free voice reaches beyond unwanted merges:

- It can spend the routine budget.
- It sends the owner’s speech, and potentially nearby conversation, to the voice provider.
- It can send repository material to model providers.
- It can fill queues and reduce availability.

The surrounding document states all of those risks. Therefore E is honest as the record of the specific decision not to require an intent-confirmation tap before merging. The wider acceptances are recorded under D and the residual-risk list.

However, its promise that a mistaken voice command can cause only source-reversible changes is true only after admission is owner-controlled, current, and checked against the proposed change. As written, that boundary can become stale or potentially be edited away.

## What remains, ranked

1. Owner-controlled, continuously valid admission — design change

Only the authenticated owner may alter admissions, the repository allowlist, connected-tool approvals, or the EMR refusal. These controls must be outside agent-writable material. The broker must refuse when the live repository state differs from the recorded proof or when a proposed change would introduce deployment, publishing, privileged automation, or other external consequences.

2. Gauntlet before the first consequential repository action — design change

The exact version reviewed must pass before its first branch push. No automation may run unreviewed agent code. The manual-deploy exception must gate the actual deployment, not merely document owner involvement somewhere in the process.

3. A genuinely narrower GitHub action path — design change

Because GitHub’s own permission bundles push/merge authority with release authority, the broker needs a separate, independently enforced operation filter. Then the build must attack that filter and prove release creation and automation-triggering calls are impossible.

4. Correct the two remaining overstatements — wording change

Replace “real money” with spending beyond the fixed limits, and stop saying the extra autonomy risk falls almost entirely on merges and spend.

Once those are added, I would regard the remaining work—VM escape attempts, credential searches, live-record migration, exact-version binding, branch-protection attacks, spend races, hostile voice tests, and verified removal of the old paths—as build-phase proof rather than further architectural debate.
